- 2026-10-26 21:03 UTC: Goal written from the owner's words. Lane 12 of the audit reviews the programs and the terminal plumbing.
- 2026-10-26 23:34 UTC: The design and the owner's decisions are in; the goal is READY. Building waits for the owner's word: the owner set this phase to documentation and planning only.
- 2026-10-27 00:07 UTC: The audit's lane 14 studied the owner's two open items, a terminal for Pi and a full usage window; its findings and nine questions, each with a recommendation, are under Open questions for the owner.
- 2026-10-27 00:13 UTC: The owner took lane 14's nine recommendations; goal 169 has no open question. Building waits for the owner's word.
- 2026-10-29 06:14 UTC: The spike is done (its report and fixtures are in `.internal/`): Claude Code 2.1.308, Codex 0.162.1 and OpenCode 2.1.4 each ran its TUI as `lane-1` under the broker and the GPU fence, in a pseudo-terminal (`node-pty` 1.1.0) with `@xterm/headless` 6.0.0, against a scripted stand-in model, and none left a screen waiting once seeded; every decision can come from the session log, the hooks or OpenCode's server, never the screen. Found: Claude Code's first message must follow `--`; its token on file descriptor 3 needs the host to spawn the program on a pseudo-terminal pair itself (`setsid -c`), since `node-pty`'s spawn passes no descriptor; its transcript gives a usage limit's reset as a number (`resetsAt`, `rateLimitType`) and records typed text taken in mid-turn; its hooks (Stop's block, PostToolUse's context) steer in the TUI; it has a messaging socket the review did not know, left closed. Codex's rollout is written line by line, its hooks match Claude Code's, and an interrupt leaves its command running. OpenCode's session-log route gives nothing usable at 2.1.4: its event stream (`/api/event`, or the plugin) and its messages route are the record, its prompt route steers or queues, and its server outlives the TUI. The TUI costs 170 to 300 MiB a program (OpenCode's server and TUI about 450), the host 60 to 100 MiB; the Pi view (`pi-tui` 1.4.0) 60 MiB. Offline, Claude Code's TUI sends the same requests as `-p` but 1.4% larger, plus a title call per typed prompt. Next: the session-log adapters, beside today's stream readers, with the spike's logs as fixtures.
- 2026-10-29 06:48 UTC: The session-log adapters are built beside today's stream readers: `src/agents/programs/session-log.ts` (the events, the adapter interface, a tally with `CliTally`'s counts) and one pure adapter for each program, `claude-code/transcript.ts`, `codex/rollout.ts` and `opencode/session-events.ts` (OpenCode's events from `/api/event` or a plugin, plus its messages route). Each gives usage once (per Claude Code message id, Codex response and OpenCode step) and counts every kind of record it does not know. Tests read the spike's logs, trimmed into `tests/fixtures/session-logs/` with Claude Code's prompts, tool descriptions and requests replaced: turns, tools, steers in mid-turn, interrupts, usage limits with their reset, resumed sessions; in `-p`, the transcript's tally equals the stream-json reader's. At the end of each headless Codex trial the harness now reads the thread's rollout and logs where it and Codex's output differ. Claude Code's transcript is private to the lane's user and headless OpenCode writes no log, so both wait for the terminal mode. Found: Claude Code's transcript leaves out the terminal UI's title calls, which its exit totals count; today's OpenCode stream reader counts only uncached input tokens in a trial's tally, where the other programs count every input token. Next: hook signals and steering inside a turn, in headless mode.
- 2026-10-29 07:37 UTC: Hook signals and the owner's messages inside a turn work in headless mode. One hook program for Claude Code and Codex (`src/agents/programs/hooks/main.ts`, Node's modules only) and Thermite's OpenCode plugin write each signal of the program to the trial's signals file; the harness follows it and reads it into the session-log adapters' events (a `hookSignals` adapter, and OpenCode's own): a signal is a hint only. An owner's message to a running trial goes in inside the agent's turn through two routes bound to the trial's token (`/v1/trial/turn/offer`, `/v1/trial/turn/taken`): Claude Code's and Codex's PostToolUse hook (added context) and Stop hook (a block, so the program goes on), and the plugin's `session.prompt` with `delivery: "steer"` when a tool starts. It goes one message at a time, rendered by its role and read once the program has it; its receipt says how (`agent_message_read.how`), and it is kept as a later turn with its place. A message that can't go in waits for the continuation, as before, and none goes in after a submission. Codex's trials run with `--dangerously-bypass-hook-trust`, since `codex exec` runs no untrusted hook. The stand-ins run the hooks as the programs do, and the Claude Code and Codex acceptance checks send two messages to a running trial and check that both go in, with their `how`. The trial's credential is already in each program's environment for `thermite-trial`, so the hooks need nothing new. Not yet run on the real programs: OpenCode's plugin `session.prompt` on 2.1.4, and Claude Code's and Codex's hook answers in `-p` and `exec` (the spike tested them in the TUIs). Next: the terminal host.
- 2026-10-29 08:53 UTC: The terminal host is built (`src/harness/terminal/`), with node-pty 1.1.0 (optional, built in the harness image's last stage with the Node stage's node-gyp and headers mounted; control and the supervisor leave it out), `@xterm/headless` 6.0.0 and `@xterm/addon-serialize` 0.14.0, pinned and credited. The broker runs it as the lane's user and spares it with the program over the memory limit (`spareRoot`). It writes the program's seeds, starts OpenCode's server first, starts the program on a pseudo-terminal of its own through `setsid -c` with Claude Code's token on file descriptor 3, keeps the screen in the emulator (text and snapshot), copies the session log into a file the harness reads, types text (then Enter apart, its control characters taken out), sends the owner's named keys, and interrupts and ends each program its own way (`/exit`, SIGTERM, then the broker). A resumed session is a new host that goes on in the same copy. The harness asks it over a socket in the run's area that answers only requests with a secret given on the host's stdin. Each program's terminal profile (command line with `--`, resume, seeds, session-log place, interrupt, end) is in its folder's `terminal.ts`. Tests: the host on a fake TUI (`tests/helpers/fake-tui.ts`) on Linux; `scripts/acceptance/terminal.mjs` passed in the image under the broker as lane-1 with the fake TUI, the real Codex 0.162.1 and OpenCode 2.1.4; Claude Code's part waits for an image built with it. Nothing starts the host yet. Next: the terminal mode in the trial loop.
- 2026-10-29 09:49 UTC: The terminal mode is in the one trial loop (`src/harness/agents/cli/terminal.ts`). `program_mode`, `terminal` or `headless`, is a rig setting: on Edit rig under **Advanced** (**Agent programs run**), and through `rig_settings` over REST, MCP and `thermite command`; headless by default until the stand-ins and acceptance checks cover the terminal. In its terminal, a trial's Claude Code, Codex or OpenCode starts through the terminal host as the lane's user, its first message on its command line and each later turn typed once the one before has ended. The tally (turns, tool calls, usage, last text, session) comes from the session log: Claude Code's transcript and Codex's rollout as the host copies them, OpenCode's events from Thermite's plugin. A turn's end comes from the log, settled for 1 s, never the screen. The owner's messages go in inside the turn as headless. A program that exits is resumed in a new host with its session. At the trial's end, a stop or a submission, a screen snapshot (at most 1 MiB) goes into the trial's area and the program is ended its own way. Claude Code's version is read once per harness start for its seeds. Tests: the loop on the fake TUI, Linux only (passed in the image), and the setting on Edit rig, REST, MCP and the command line; `scripts/acceptance/terminal.mjs` passed in the image with one trial through the loop for the fake TUI, the real Codex 0.162.1 and OpenCode 2.1.4. Not yet: the real Claude Code through the loop; the screen snapshot is removed with the trial's area, so the live view's step keeps it elsewhere. Next: the stand-ins' terminal modes and the acceptance checks in the terminal, then the default flipped to `terminal`; the planner and the advisors in their terminals.
- 2026-10-29 12:34 UTC: The terminal is the default: `PROGRAM_MODE_DEFAULT` is `terminal`, a rig's settings file keeps only what someone set, so every rig nobody set follows it, and a report without `program_mode` still reads as headless; Edit rig lists **In their own terminal** first. The stand-ins run in their own terminal as the real programs do: `containers/test-claude` (without `-p`: a screen, its first message after `--`, typed text and pastes, Esc, `/exit`, its transcript as Claude Code 2.1.308 writes it, 0600, with `turn_duration`, a Stop hook's block and `cost-state`, its hooks, and `--version`) and `containers/test-codex` (without `exec`: its rollout as Codex 0.162.1 writes it, line by line, its hooks and notify, `/stop`, `/exit`, `resume`). The Claude Code and Codex acceptance checks run their first trial in the terminal on a rig nobody set and, the rig set to headless over REST, a later trial headless, each with the owner's two messages inside its turn; the agents acceptance runs OpenCode's hill-climb trial in its terminal and its research trial headless; the real Claude Code acceptance runs the same trial in its terminal and headless, and the trial that compacts in its terminal. Memory in the terminal, the host included: Claude Code 553 and 574 MiB at a trial's peak with its commands (342 and 346 headless), Codex 304 to 307 MiB, OpenCode 636 to 640 MiB; the limits notes say about 550, 300 and 650 MiB. Passed: `terminal.mjs` (an image with Claude Code 2.1.308), `claude-real.mjs`, `claude.mjs`, `codex.mjs`, `agents.mjs`; the full suite (2,098 and 154, none failing). Found: Codex's terminal takes no `--ignore-rules` (an `exec` flag), so a project's execpolicy rules load there. Next: the planner and the advisors in their terminals, the steering command (`terminal_input`), the live view, usage from the terminal.

